From 5920c6ee144fffff84b62f5b479171f734f15316 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peter Hutterer <peter.hutterer@who-t.net>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 15:24:17 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] encparse: fix heap out-of-bounds write in setCode()

setCode() computes a destination index from two attacker-controlled
values taken from the .enc encoding file:

    index = (from >> 8) * row_size + (from & 0xFF);

The "from" parameter is <= 0xFFFF (see the check a few lines above), so
from >> 8 can reach 0xFF. row_size comes straight from the SIZE line of
the encoding file.

The destination buffer is allocated with a size capped at 0x10000
entries. At row_size > 256, the index exceeds the allocation, causing a
heap out-of-bounds write.

For example, SIZE 256 258 and mapping 0xff00 -> 0 gives:
  index = 255 * 258 + 0 = 65790, allocation = 65536 entries

Additionally, the column bounds check incorrectly tested the global
variable "value1" instead of the function parameter "from".  For
CODE_LINE this happens to be the same value, but for CODE_RANGE_LINE
and CODE_UNDEFINE_LINE the loop calls setCode() with different values
of "from" while value1 remains unchanged.

This vulnerability was discovered by:
  Adam Bedard working with TrendAI Zero Day Initiative

CVE-2026-109908, ZDI-CAN-31888

Assisted-by: AI
Part-of: <https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/xorg/lib/libfontenc/-/merge_requests/20>
---
 src/encparse.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/encparse.c b/src/encparse.c
index 39dbc89..8949fd4 100644
--- a/src/encparse.c
+++ b/src/encparse.c
@@ -455,9 +455,11 @@ setCode(unsigned from, unsigned to, unsigned row_size,
     if (row_size == 0)
         index = from;
     else {
-        if ((value1 & 0xFF) >= row_size)
+        if ((from & 0xFF) >= row_size)
             return 0;           /* ignore out of range mappings */
         index = (from >> 8) * row_size + (from & 0xFF);
+        if (index > 0xFFFF)
+            return 0;           /* ignore mappings that exceed the encoding size */
     }
 
     /* Optimize away useless identity mappings.  This is only expected
